+# The Vet Cache Excludes Filtered Reads
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Both `VetRepository` reads are cached in the `vets` cache, which has no size limit and no eviction. The specialty filter adds reads keyed by a caller-supplied string on open routes. Every route is reachable by any caller, and a name matching nothing is a normal HTTP 200 result.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Cache filtered reads as the existing reads are cached** - Rejected: each distinct value adds an entry for the life of the process, so any caller can grow the heap without limit.
+2. **Bound the cache through a provider-specific configuration** - Rejected: the JCache API carries no size limit, so this needs a provider dependency for a data set treated as bounded.
+3. **Cache only values matching a known specialty** - Rejected: the check is itself a query, and it adds a path to reason about.
+4. **Leave filtered reads uncached** (chosen) - Each filtered read costs one query over a bounded data set.
+
+## Decision
+
+The `vets` cache holds unfiltered reads only. A repository read keyed by caller-supplied text carries no cache annotation. The page number on the unfiltered read stays as it is.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Distinct filter values add no retained memory.
+- Every filtered request reaches the database. The rows in § Scale and Load treat that cost as bounded.
+- A later filter on the directory inherits this rule.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § Scale and Load](../system-design.md#scale-and-load)
+- [system-design.md § Threat Model](../system-design.md#threat-model)
